eral caucus  there is enormous room for respectful  conversation for differences of opinion  late thursday green party leader anime  paul expressed disappointment at atwin's  departure  but doesn't believe it's a reflection on  her leadership  she wished me well that she hoped that  we would remain friends  and so i will take her at her word that  this is a decision she took  for other reasons the timing of this  couldn't be worse for the greens the  party is losing its star  mp while planning for the possibility of  a fall election  donna all right michael couture thanks  canada's privacy commissioner has  concluded the rcmp seriously breached  privacy laws  by using facial recognition software to  collect the personal  information of canadians the privacy  commissioner daniel therion tabled his  report in parliament today  after investigating the rcmp's use of  the software from  the u.s space company clearview ai the  software cross-references  images of people against billions of  photos taken from the internet without  their consent  the commissioner concluded the use of  facial recognition technology by the  rcmp  to search through massive repositories  of canadians who are innocent  of any suspicion of crime presents a  serious violation of privacy  the rcmp at first denied using clearview  ai but last year admitted  it had been it's still not clear what  for  the domino is now falling that keystone  xl is dead  coming up what it means for alberta's  premier and the taxpayer dollars  he invested  it's been a bad week for alberta premier  jason kenney  first he apologized for floating coveted  rules at a dinner on the sky palace  balcony  new polling shows his approval rating at  a new low  and now the keystone xl pipeline  expansion which he invested in  is officially dead it was first proposed  in 2008  the 1900 kilometer pipeline would have  carried crude from alberta to nebraska  and then tied in with an  existing pipeline carrying crude to the  u.s gulf coast  just last year kenny announced a 1.5  billion dollar investment in the project  and  six billion dollars in loan guarantees  president biden pulled the permits for  the project and yesterday  tc energy canceled it global's tom  vernon is with me  from edmonton tom before we get to  keystone i want to ask you about a  report in an alberta online publication  that premier kenny and others in his  cabinet broke covid19 rules by having  multiple dinners indoors kenny was asked  about that today what did he say  well donna a digital media outlet quotes  unnamed sources inside the ucp  government  that accused the premier of hosting  these dinners at restaurants as well as  at a private resident  while the restrictions were in place not  allowing this to happen now the premier  was  quick to deny these allegations and they  sent a cease and desist letter  to the outlet this is a complete  fabrication and that's why  our legal counsel has written to that  website  demanding a full and immediate  retraction and apology  now the premier global news has not  verified these allegations now the  premier did add that he has had  meals out at restaurants during the  pandemic while the rules did allow to  show support for the industry  he did so either on his own or with one  of his designated close contacts  okay and tom back to keystone now what  happens to the taxpayer money that kenny  invested in and what are the possible  political consequences  yeah donna as of right now alberta  taxpayers are on the hook for about 1.3  billion dollars that's according to the  premier and  the government and tc energy are looking  for ways to recoup some of that cost  from the biden administration on the  premier says alberta has a case to make  under the free trade agreement between  the two countries and we'll learn more  about that  towards the end of july now this comes  as an angus reed poll  shows the premier has the lowest support  for all premiers in the country at 31  and if 